<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today's Weather Will Be Sunny with Some Rain Later.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: productive: Create instructions for using a tool or device.<br>interactive: Respond to peer writing.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write instructions for using a weather app (e.g., 'Open the app and check the temperature.').<br>Can list items to bring, based on weather predictions (e.g., 'Bring sunscreen and water if it's sunny.').<br>Can respond to peer comments on trip plans (e.g., 'It is a good idea to bring umbrellas in case it rains.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) on the board or screen. Students are asked to write down one adjective and one noun to describe each picture (e.g., 'sunny day', 'cloudy sky', 'windy storm'). Then, the teacher elicits oral sentences from students describing the pictures using "It is..." and introduces "It will be..." for predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple instructional sentences on the board using imperative verbs related to a weather app (e.g., 'Open the app.', 'Check the temperature.', 'Look at the forecast.'). The teacher also models writing a simple weather prediction sentence using future simple: 'Tomorrow, it will be sunny with a warm breeze.' and a recommendation: 'You should bring a hat.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Open the app,' am I telling you to do something now or later?" (Now)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'will be' tell us about the weather?" (It's a prediction for the future.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'thermostat' by segmenting it ('ther-mo-stat') during a quick oral review of vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, written text titled "How to Use a Basic Weather App" along with a simple weather forecast report for the next day. The text includes instructions and predictions, using future simple tense, prepositions of place, modal verbs for recommendations, and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">"<b>How to Use Our Weather App:</b><br>1. Open the app on your phone.<br>2. Look for your city. It is in the center of the screen.<br>3. Check the temperature. It will be 25 degrees.<br>4. See the forecast for tomorrow. It will be sunny in the morning with a clear sky, and cloudy in the afternoon. There might be some rain over the lake in the evening. You should bring an umbrella."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting written instructions and simple weather reports to extract specific information and prepare for written production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer written questions based on the text:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match the instruction to the weather app's function (e.g., 'Check the temperature' → '25 degrees').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: 'Tomorrow, it will be rainy all day.' (False, it will be sunny in the morning).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Complete the sentence: 'The rain is ____ the lake.' (over)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using future simple tense for weather predictions, prepositions of place, and modal verbs for recommendations. Focus on correct spelling of weather vocabulary (nouns: forecast, report, storm, temperature; verbs: will be, predict, bring; adjectives: sunny, cloudy, rainy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using the correct form of verbs, prepositions, or target vocabulary words.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Tomorrow, the weather _____ (be) _____ (adjective: sunny/cloudy)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The wind is strong _____ (preposition: in/on/at) the morning."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "It looks like rain; you _____ (modal: should/will) bring an umbrella."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Guided Sentence Writing):</b> Students are given prompts (e.g., 'rain, tomorrow, bring raincoat', 'sun, afternoon, wear hat'). They write complete sentences incorporating future simple, modals, and appropriate vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "Tomorrow / wind / strong / morning" → Student writes: "Tomorrow, the wind will be strong in the morning."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "Afternoon / sunny / bring / water" → Student writes: "In the afternoon, it will be sunny. You should bring water."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages students to pay attention to intonation when reading their written predictions aloud, emphasizing the last word of the sentence (e.g., 'It will be **sunny**.') to reinforce the pragmatic function of describing weather conditions.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Practice (Peer Sentence Check):</b> In pairs, students write 3-4 sentences predicting the weather for their local area tomorrow, including at least one item they should bring. They then exchange their written sentences with their partner and check for correct use of future simple, modal verbs, prepositions of place, and spelling of vocabulary. They write a short, constructive comment (e.g., "Good use of 'will be'!" or "Check the spelling of 'umbrella'."). This encourages sociolinguistic interaction through written feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product. Students will create two distinct written pieces based on the theme "Today's Weather Will Be Sunny with Some Rain Later."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 1: Create Weather App Instructions.</b> Students write 3-4 simple, clear instructions for using a weather app, using imperative verbs and integrating target vocabulary (e.g., 'open', 'check', 'temperature', 'forecast', 'look', 'sky', 'sunshine'). They can also include a short prediction within the instructions (e.g., "Open the app. Check the temperature; it will be warm."). This directly addresses the specific objective: 'Create instructions for using a tool or device.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 2: Write Weather-Based Recommendations.</b> Based on a given (or imagined) weather prediction for tomorrow (e.g., "Tomorrow, it will be sunny in the morning, but cloudy with rain in the afternoon."), students write a short paragraph listing 3-4 items people should bring or wear. They must use the future simple tense for predictions, modal verbs for recommendations ('should bring'), and prepositions of place if relevant (e.g., 'The rain is in the east'). This fulfills the learning outcome: 'Can list items to bring, based on weather predictions.'</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support with vocabulary and grammar, ensuring students are focused on producing clear written output that incorporates the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written instructions and recommendations from Stage 4 with a different partner. Each student reads their partner's writing and writes 1-2 constructive comments or questions on a sticky note (e.g., "It is a good idea to bring an umbrella in case it rains." or "Is the temperature for the morning or afternoon?"). This provides an opportunity for interactive peer writing response, aligning with the specific objective. The teacher collects all written work (instructions, recommendations, and peer comments) for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successfully creates 3-4 step instructions for a weather app.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses future simple tense ('will be') for weather predictions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least 3-4 target weather vocabulary words (e.g., sunny, rain, temperature, forecast, bring, umbrella, sunscreen).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal verbs ('should') for recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instructions are clear and easy to follow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to peer writing with relevant and polite comments.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I wrote well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I need to practice more in my writing is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel writing instructions for a simple tool now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new weather word did I use the most in my writing today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this lesson to support students' writing skills? (e.g., the structured practice, the clear models, peer feedback)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially regarding grammar accuracy (future simple, modals) or vocabulary use in writing?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students engage effectively in responding to peer writing, and was their feedback constructive?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written weather app instructions and reports to explain information to a classmate or prepare a simple presentation, integrating their writing skills with mediation and communicative project work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>